WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:02.407
[screaming]

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.100
No. A helicopter cannot fly 90 degrees

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.060
on its side like that.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:07.893
That's crazy.

00:00:07.893 --> 00:00:08.810
Hey, fly peeps.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.342
I am Vernice "FlyGirl"
Armour coming at you.

00:00:11.342 --> 00:00:13.070
And back in 2003,

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:16.580
I became America's first
Black female combat pilot,

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.510
serving two tours in Iraq.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.220
Today, I'll be looking at
helicopter scenes in movies

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.720
and judging how real they are.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:26.977
Man: It's too much weight!

00:00:26.977 --> 00:00:28.235
All right, drop them!

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.990
An attack helicopter, on average,

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.320
can only handle so much weight.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.680
When I was going in combat,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.330
sometimes we couldn't even
carry the full amount of fuel

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:40.490
because we were carrying
more rockets and missiles.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.830
So the weight of that semi

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.720
well is beyond the 2,000 to 4,000 payload,

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.790
let alone the other vehicles
that were attached to it.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:48.932
Totally unrealistic.

00:00:53.398 --> 00:00:54.760
I think they were trying to simulate

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.190
the helicopter working so
hard against the vehicles

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.715
to not be pulled,

00:00:58.715 --> 00:01:00.300
but that just wouldn't happen.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.690
When a helicopter is pulling
more weight than it can,

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.120
the engine isn't going
to blow out like that.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.770
It's just going to slow
down the transmission,

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:11.510
and the helicopter just
won't be able to lift.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:15.379
It's going to drag it down,
but it wouldn't explode.

00:01:15.379 --> 00:01:17.270
Man: Sir, the weapons are back online.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:18.106
Goodbye.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.610
A missile or rocket actually has to arm,

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.300
and there was absolutely not enough time

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.230
for a missile to be able to come off.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.610
Any shot where you're
going to shoot something

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.426
within 50 feet, basically,

00:01:32.426 --> 00:01:34.660
50 to 100 feet, unrealistic.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.100
And if they did get it off,
it's not going to explode.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.558
It's just going to be a chunk of metal,

00:01:38.558 --> 00:01:39.940
for the most part, that hits the ground.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.960
And if it did explode,

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.390
the shrapnel is -- the
metal from the ordnance

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.560
is going to come up and
impact the helicopter,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.480
which would probably take it down.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.710
I'd give it a 3.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.510
Different missiles arm
at different distances,

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:54.768
but not within 50 feet.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.721
[alarm beeping]

00:02:00.721 --> 00:02:02.340
Alarm: Pull up. Pull up.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.806
So, the descending spiral happens

00:02:04.806 --> 00:02:06.722
when there's more torque

00:02:06.722 --> 00:02:08.650
on a certain part of the helicopter.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.460
That's why there's the tail rotor,

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:12.330
'cause if there was only
the rotor at the top,

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:13.450
the helicopter would spin,

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.280
so the rotor at the back
keeps it from spinning.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.080
So that meant something
was out of balance.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.440
Could he get it back in control?

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.760
Mm, yes, absolutely.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.410
I didn't see any real reason

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.987
on why the helicopter was nosediving.

00:02:26.987 --> 00:02:28.445
And as soon as you pull up

00:02:28.445 --> 00:02:30.461
and the rotor blades switch position,

00:02:30.461 --> 00:02:32.490
it should automatically

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.200
pitch the nose of the aircraft up,

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.600
whether the engine is running or not.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.180
That, just aerodynamically,
that's going to happen.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.250
Why he couldn't pull the
nose up, I'm not sure,

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.700
unless he had a hydraulics malfunction

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.960
in which the controls weren't responding.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:48.220
But that wasn't the case,

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.430
because at a certain point
he pulled the stick up

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.923
and he flew off, so.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:53.755
Unrealistic.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:00.970
Unless the guy's just a horrible shot,

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.750
helicopters just don't maneuver superfast.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.880
There's no way to get
out of bullets like that.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.390
A helicopter doesn't react like a jet.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:10.850
There's a little bit of a lag.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.140
So if I were to do the
stick back and forth,

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.550
my helicopter, it would
rotate on its axis,

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:17.698
but it wouldn't have necessarily

00:03:17.698 --> 00:03:20.620
had time to move left
or time to move right.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.730
So you would see it dip and come back up,

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.420
but it wouldn't have necessarily moved

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.430
out of the line of fire that quickly.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.180
Just doesn't happen like with a jet.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.680
Immediately, it'll fly off
to the left or the right.

00:03:39.496 --> 00:03:41.137
[laughs] Yeah.

00:03:41.137 --> 00:03:43.595
No one would've survived that.

00:03:43.595 --> 00:03:45.960
That cabin, that helicopter,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.180
the frame would not even be intact

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.860
by any stretch of the imagination.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.370
Totally destroyed as
soon as the blades hit.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.145
And that showed it rolling.

00:03:56.145 --> 00:03:57.645
It would have been an explosion.

00:03:57.645 --> 00:04:00.150
The gas is right inside of the helicopter.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.780
Yeah, no. That's the impossible, I guess.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.730
When your aircraft is going down,

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:08.030
you're actually flying the aircraft down.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.010
There's this thing called an autorotation,

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.790
which, when you have a fan,
and it's just in the window

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:14.500
and a breeze blows through the window

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:15.870
and the fan starts turning,

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:18.060
that's the basics of a rotor.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.430
Well, when you're in the
aircraft and it's coming down,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.000
basically falling out of the sky,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.240
let's say you've lost power to the engine,

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.980
it's just a big fan at the top.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.222
So a few feet above the ground,

00:04:28.222 --> 00:04:29.480
you pull up the collective

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.410
while it's still flying a little forward,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.820
and that resistance can
cushion the landing.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:35.930
In this case, there was no cushion,

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:39.270
there was no controlled descent.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.690
It was catastrophic. There
wouldn't have been anything left.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:43.265
I'd have to give it a 1.

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:49.600
You can't fly through something like that.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.520
Unrealistic.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.030
Rotors would've already
fallen off the aircraft.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.170
As hard as rotor blades look,

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:59.730
they're very sensitive and
finely tuned and balanced.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.760
And as soon as it impacts an object,

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.325
you're not going to be able to
fly the aircraft after that.

00:05:04.325 --> 00:05:05.401
Hurry up!

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:08.930
Because it's The Rock,
could've been realistic

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:10.130
with him hanging on the side.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.870
It's like hanging on to a car, right?

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:14.170
Because most of the downward push

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:15.910
of the wind from a helicopter

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:19.140
is on the last third of the blades.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.910
It's not in the very center.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.290
So, the wind shear

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:25.256
wouldn't have pushed
him out of the middle.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.000
Totally unrealistic.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.220
It's just like the wheel of a car.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.800
It's never in one position,
it's constantly moving.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.220
Where'd he get the piece
of metal to even fix it?

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.250
When they showed it in that
clip, it was just stable.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.170
He's actually able to
hold it, and the rod,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.277
and it's just not going
to be stable like that.

00:05:43.277 --> 00:05:44.552
[screaming]

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.060
No. A helicopter cannot fly

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.630
90 degrees on its side like that.

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:50.463
That's crazy.

00:05:50.463 --> 00:05:52.866
I would give it a 1.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.620
Guns at the front of
the attack helicopters,

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:01.453
is that realistic?

00:06:01.453 --> 00:06:02.286
Absolutely.

00:06:02.286 --> 00:06:05.000
I had a 20-millimeter
Gatling gun, three barrel.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:09.280
It rolled around and
delivered lots of bullets

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:12.680
very fast on target,
so, yes, 100% realistic.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.480
Only the pilots would fire that weapon.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.340
You see two rocket
launchers left to right,

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:21.440
then two missile launchers left and right.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:24.630
There are 26 rockets in
each one of those pods,

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.960
then there are four
missiles on the end board.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.270
That is actually a standard payload

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:31.445
for an attack helicopter.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.450
The rockets are called 2.75-inch rockets.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:44.100
So they're about this big
around, right, 2.75 inches,

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.810
and they're pretty long.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.930
The rockets are just like bullets.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:50.580
You aim, you shoot, you pull,

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:53.640
they go wherever, wind shear impacts them.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:57.920
The Hellfire missiles
are laser-guided, right?

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:01.030
So they're guided by laser
to a particular target.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:03.140
A rocket impacts a small area,

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:05.380
let's say it's 20 feet, 30 feet.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:08.000
These rockets are
basically just, you know,

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.200
as soon as they hit, they
explode, and it's a point target.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.041
The missile has

00:07:13.041 --> 00:07:16.541
a lot more explosive power to it.

00:07:16.541 --> 00:07:19.200
You could shoot a tank or a
building. It's going to explode.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.490
There are heat-seeking missiles

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:26.090
and missiles that can fly to
catch a jet, per se, right?

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:27.890
But those aren't the kind of missiles

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:29.740
that, No. 1, were on this aircraft,

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:34.220
and, No. 2, helicopters
don't normally carry them.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:38.227
And they don't

00:07:38.227 --> 00:07:39.255
follow humans.

00:07:39.255 --> 00:07:42.810
And I doubt his jet pack
had enough heat coming off,

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.710
'cause it probably would
have burned up his legs

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:45.960
if that were the case.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.720
There would have been no
way they could just glide

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.850
into going through the
vortices of the helicopter.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:59.388
I could easily see them being blown down

00:07:59.388 --> 00:08:01.890
50 to 100 feet [snaps] just like that.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.410
The payload, that was actually realistic.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.380
The heat-seeking missiles, not so much.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:07.880
I'm going to give it a 7.

00:08:14.732 --> 00:08:17.210
Flying in formation in
helicopters is very common.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:18.450
We practice it a lot.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.790
We will land together,
we'll take off together.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:22.210
And in an environment like this,

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:24.055
where you have that mission
where you're dropping off,

00:08:24.055 --> 00:08:27.952
say, a flight of soldiers in this case,

00:08:27.952 --> 00:08:30.583
yeah, that would absolutely
happen like that.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:40.460
Just everything about
that combat maneuver,

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:42.320
the insertion, them dropping,

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.060
that's why we don't do static things

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.870
like that anymore in combat,

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:49.350
because of the vulnerability.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.158
And the helicopter has pretty much

00:08:52.158 --> 00:08:55.080
no maneuverability when
you're in that close

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.340
to be able to get out really quick.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:58.060
A helicopter just doesn't move quick.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:00.534
A helicopter has to build up momentum

00:09:00.534 --> 00:09:03.067
and get going, right?

00:09:03.067 --> 00:09:06.520
RPGs are a huge threat to helicopters,

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.430
especially if we're flying
what we call low and slow.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.360
But I remember flying in
combat, and we just saw,

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:12.740
and it was early on in combat,

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:14.650
and I just saw these gray clouds of mist,

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:15.690
and we were trying to figure out, like,

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:17.060
what are those clouds of mist?

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:19.886
And they were RPGs that
had been programmed

00:09:19.886 --> 00:09:23.067
to explode at 200 and 300 feet,

00:09:23.067 --> 00:09:26.310
and some of my buddies
were impacted by RPGs.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:27.340
Huge threat.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:36.860
So, the one, I would say,
unrealistic part of that clip,

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:41.200
when that RPG hit the tail
rotor of that aircraft,

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:44.200
I thought it would've been
spinning a lot faster,

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:47.570
and it wasn't gliding down without power.

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:49.320
And at the very end,

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.920
if he tried to cushion
the landing a little bit,

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.150
which we're taught to do
in that kind of situation,

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:55.550
with a loss of tail rotor,

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:57.500
we would've tried to circle around

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.280
in the direction that it's spinning

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:01.010
to have a little control.

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:03.380
But it wouldn't have just landed flat

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.030
like you saw in that video.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.560
So, the guys in the back,
hopefully they're tied in,

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.290
or they would just
immediately be thrown out.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:11.010
Just like a car accident that twirls over

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:13.210
and everybody gets ejected
out of the vehicle.

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:14.810
The reason, I think,

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:17.960
they show or film more helos

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:19.085
getting hit in the tail rotor

00:10:19.085 --> 00:10:20.260
is just 'cause it's more dramatic.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:21.410
You get hit in the tail rotor,

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:23.100
you're immediately going to start spinning

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:24.280
and go out of control.

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:25.720
If you get in the main rotor,

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.310
you just explode and fall out the sky.

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:28.820
Which one's more exciting?

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:30.736
On a scale of 1 to 10,

00:10:30.736 --> 00:10:31.602
9.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:37.400
My only thing would be right here.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.920
Why is the helicopter out of control?

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.400
Nobody's touching the pilot.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.370
There're just two guys
fighting in the back.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:46.460
It wouldn't be hard to
fight and move around

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:47.630
in the back of an aircraft.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:51.200
It would almost be like
an elevator without walls.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:53.017
Do not do a barrel roll.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.930
Oh, my God. [laughs] It's so -- no.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:01.180
An extended turn at a large degree,

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:04.840
it would just have too much
weight lost off of the rotor.

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:08.960
A helicopter is pulling
on this small little pole.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.590
There always needs to be
weight on the rotor system

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:14.350
from the aircraft, right?

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:16.733
So all of those little,

00:11:16.733 --> 00:11:18.860
the barrel roll he did at the end

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:20.870
and the inversion flight,

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:24.660
that's just completely
unrealistic for a helicopter.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.010
There is the Red Bull helicopter

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:30.110
that is capable of doing things like that.

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:31.310
But for the most part,

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:34.610
99% of helicopters out there

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:35.910
can't do things like this.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:39.150
You would never want to
go past 50, 60 degrees.

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:43.670
I'd have to give it a ...

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:44.796
3.

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:55.936
Countries have different helicopters,

00:11:55.936 --> 00:11:57.710
and this was foreign.

00:11:57.710 --> 00:12:00.910
Like, definitely had a lot more stability,

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:03.500
'cause it had those kind
of semi-wings, right?

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:06.130
It could carry a lot more armament.

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:08.110
With attack helicopters here in America,

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:09.900
we have the Apache in the Army

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:12.730
and the Cobra in the
Marine Corps. Very similar.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:17.405
It's like comparing the
Mustang to the Camaro, right?

00:12:17.405 --> 00:12:18.730
There are going to be
a lot of similarities,

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:20.420
but there are also going
to be some differences.

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:22.300
What you'll see in attack helicopters

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.280
is you'll have the tandem,

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:27.990
which is pilot, copilot; front, back.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:31.230
Or you'll have side by side,

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:32.420
as in the Black Hawks.

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:34.700
And the Apache is actually tandem as well,

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:35.750
just like the Cobra.

00:12:39.839 --> 00:12:40.760
[screams]

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:43.220
When you're flying a helicopter

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.510
that actually has what
we call door gunners,

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:46.380
right, in the back,

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:48.870
the pilots know how to
maneuver the aircraft

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:51.440
to get the target in the right place

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:54.150
for the door gunners to be
able to shoot at that target.

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:55.790
It's Rambo. Not sure how much training

00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:58.050
he's gone through as a pilot, right,

00:12:58.050 --> 00:12:58.883
with door gunners.

00:12:58.883 --> 00:13:00.490
But you get it in position,

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:02.100
and the door gunners
take care of business.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.920
The crew chief would say,
"Come left," or, "Come right."

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:06.378
Or, you know, they would give them

00:13:06.378 --> 00:13:08.003
very specific instructions,

00:13:08.003 --> 00:13:09.870
especially if their
environment is changing

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:11.100
and it's over on the right side

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:13.140
but the left-seat pilot
can't see it, right?

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.830
So they're in constant
communication the entire time.

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:17.872
I'll give it a 6.

00:13:18.988 --> 00:13:20.829
Fire the missiles!

00:13:20.829 --> 00:13:22.195
Come on, come on.

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:24.206
We go cold!

00:13:24.206 --> 00:13:25.456
[missiles boom]

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:29.200
Yeah, there wasn't much
realistic about that clip

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:30.480
at all, whatsoever.

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:33.460
Let's say the heat-seeking
missile was coming after us.

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:35.830
Just when you shut the engines off,

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:37.580
the engines are still hot.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:40.240
So the heat-seeking missiles
would've still found them.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.590
There's no situation where
I would cut an engine off.

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:44.640
The rotors aren't going
to stop turning like that,

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:47.120
and just the fact that it
was flying through the air,

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:50.100
the rotor would never be not turning,

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:52.015
even if it didn't have power to it.

00:13:52.015 --> 00:13:53.498
Pilot: Restart. Restart.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:57.260
Woo-hoo!

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:01.170
They were literally 1,000
feet above the ground.

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:03.960
You're not going to get
the rotors turning again.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.800
And then fast enough to get
the aircraft back in control

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:09.080
to be able to lift it up.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:12.380
That was completely, 100%, unrealistic.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:14.400
A helicopter is not like a plane.

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:16.264
So you're not going to glide.

00:14:16.264 --> 00:14:18.714
Radio: General Tuco. You
are currently engaged

00:14:18.714 --> 00:14:21.310
in warfare on United
States military forces.

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:23.090
Helicopter-to-helicopter combat

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:25.970
is just not common at all, whatsoever,

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:27.850
because when you think about helicopters,

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:31.320
that's up close and
personal. Troops in contact.

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:34.240
The only time you've seen
helicopter versus helicopter

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:35.810
is in a movie like this,

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.530
where the bad guys are chasing
the good guys, and that's it.

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:40.580
If a helicopter is
caught up in a dogfight,

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:43.297
something has gone drastically wrong,

00:14:43.297 --> 00:14:45.110
'cause it just shouldn't happen.

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:47.230
We do have training,

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:49.600
but the training is actually focused on

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:54.560
a jet chasing us, not another helicopter.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:57.250
If we were to get into
a situation like that,

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:59.300
looks a lot like what
you see on the TV, right,

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:01.558
where you're yanking it to the left,

00:15:01.558 --> 00:15:04.170
or pushing those over,
pulling up on the collective,

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:06.010
coming up, flying around.

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:07.520
The more you're moving around,

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:09.170
you're a harder target to hit,

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:11.750
versus a straight line,
where they can track you

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:13.530
and then take you out of the sky.

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.740
Just, helicopters are very vulnerable.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:18.130
We don't move that fast.

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:21.420
So if you're caught in
that kind of a situation,

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:23.330
the odds aren't exactly with you.

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:26.200
But all the other "tricks"
and things they did,

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:27.670
just unrealistic.

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:31.460
This is just going to have to be a 2.

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:33.470
And if you enjoyed this video,

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:36.118
click the link above to watch another one.

